commentBody:
@dutchiris

The US is a big place.  I think that here in the Northeast, the two are perfect homophones.

But then again, you left coasters think that Mary, marry and merry are all pronounced the same way.

@Grumpy

Sounds very wrong to my ears, as well!! I got what they were going for, but I personally would never think those to be homophones.

But as Steve L said, it's a big place!

@Grumpy

Any pairs like those have the same distinction as Mary and merry, as exemplified by Maryanne’s pronunciation in the linked video. 

I have no idea why people would willingly create so many homophones when it’s not necessary. It’s not as if English didn’t already have so few that it needs more.

@Grumpy
In my part of Toronto all the parts of Mary being merry as she married sound different. 
Of course we’re all from somewhere else aren’t we?

@Grumpy
Since I always used to go to Canada for Spring Break when all the other students went to Florida, I picked up many pronunciation differences. I've always said "Soar-ree" when I want to be a little testy. Usually, "Well, SOAR-ee.

Also, unlike the rest of the country, as a born and bred New Yawkuh I say FLAH-rid-duh instead of FLOOR-rih-duh. (Accent on the "duh." IYKYK.) And AHR-ringe instead of OR-inge. Other than than, I've worked hard to get rid of my Brooklyn accent.

@Lewis

[“… and many of the hints are so impenetrable it makes me want to scream!]

ENRAGING CLUES ??

@Weak,

Haven’t heard “racing the engine” for REVving it in quite a while, but it’s a thing. 

Clever clue!

@Lynn

Then how have Sundays come by the reputation of being the most difficult puzzles of the week?

Have they always been Wednesday-level difficult?  Even during the Maleska years?  And before?

@Matt
During the Maleska years the difficulty did not vary by day of week. And I think Sundays were always larger, so they probably took longer even with clues of similar difficulty?

With Shortz, who introduced the easier cluing for earlier in the week, it has always been Saturdays that were the most difficult. But for the many of us outside NYC who only subscribed to the Sunday paper, the larger Sunday puzzles with their tricks and layers were the only ones we knew. Shortz published several collections of them.

@Jacqui J

